We currently permit the following:

```ts
type Wrapped<T> = {
  value: T;
}

function wrap<T>(value: T): Wrapped<T> {
  return { value };
}

const obj = wrap({
  name: wrap("thing"),
  get parent() {
    return obj;  // Recursive reference
  }
});
```

However, adding constraints in `wrap`:

```ts
type Wrapped<T> = {
  value: T;
}

function wrap<T extends Record<string, Wrapped<unknown>> | string>(value: T): Wrapped<T> {
  return { value };
}

const obj = wrap({  // Circularity error
  name: wrap("thing"),
  get parent() {  // Circularity error
    return obj;
  }
});
```

causes circularity errors to be reported. These errors have restricted correctness of type checking in common libraries like Zod that depend on this pattern.

With this PR we support the pattern even when constraints are present. We do so by suppressing constraint checks in recursive invocations of `resolveCall` that occur when checking recursive constructs. The checks are suppressed only for non-overloaded functions (since overload resolution actually depends on the outcome of constraint checks). The suppression doesn't actually affect errors associated with constraint checks since validation still occurs in the outermost invocations of `resolveCall`.

The PR includes a test that is an adaptation of the [examples provided here](https://github.com/colinhacks/recursive-inference). This test is basically `shipped.ts` with full constraint checks restored.

Fixes #64192.

A proper child generator is a good alternative to the callback-based `forEachChild` when working in async (or generator) API contexts. You can, for example:

```ts
async function *visitNodeForFirstAnyChild(node: Node): AsyncGenerator<Node | undefined, Node | undefined, Node | undefined> {
    for (const n of node.childrenIter()) {
        const t = await checker.getTypeAtLocation(n);
        if (t.flags & TypeFlags.Any) { return n; }
        const res = yield* visitNodeForFirstAnyChild(n)
        if (res) { return res; }
    }
}
const res = (await visitNodeForFirstAnyChild(stmt).next()).value;
assert.strictEqual(res!.getText(), "p: any");
```

Replaces

```diff
- api.createProgram(rootFiles, { compilerOptions, references });
+ api.createProgram(rootFiles, compilerOptions, { references });

- snapshot.update({ createPrograms: [{ rootFiles, { compilerOptions } }] });
+ snapshot.update({ createPrograms: [{ rootFiles, compilerOptions }] });
```

leaving `CreateProgramOptions` with only optional things, whereas `compilerOptions` is required.

Also fixes excess property checking and completions in `compilerOptions` in `createSnapshot` and `snapshot.update`. Those functions capture the shape of `createPrograms` and `openFiles` so that the type of `snapshot.operation.createdPrograms` and `snapshot.operation.openedFiles` can be a tuple with the correct length, but the previous type implementation of that was too open-ended.

ALSO contributes an API client skill, moved from my own user instructions, to try to get rid of those stupid `...(options ? { snapshot: options.snapshot } : {})` spreads once and for all.

Fixes #64069

This adds a way to perform isolated, arbitrary `resolveModuleName` calls, and provides two ways of overriding module resolution in API programs.

## Creating and using a module resolver

```ts
// Create a resolver with compiler options:
using resolver = api.createModuleResolver(compilerOptions);

// Resolve against the host file system:
const dirname = fileURLToPath(new URL(".", import.meta.url));
const r1 = resolver.resolveModuleName("typescript", dirname, ModuleKind.ESNext);

// Resolve against an existing snapshot:
const r2 = resolver.resolveModuleName("typescript", dirname, ModuleKind.ESNext, { snapshot });
```

